    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (6/6 tests pass).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now honours an `Idempotency-Key` header. The change slots into the existing OfficeFloor function-injection style rather than adding logic to any existing class:\n\n**New pieces (each with a single responsibility):**\n\n1. **`IdempotencyStore`** \u2014 a thread-safe, application-scoped `@Component` mapping each seen key \u2192 the id of the owner it first created (`putIfAbsent` keeps the first winner on a race). It holds ids, not entities, so no detached JPA object is carried across requests.\n\n2. **`IdempotencyKey`** \u2014 a small record carrying the header value between steps as a typed pipeline variable (avoids `Out<String>` type collisions).\n\n3. **`CheckIdempotencyKey`** \u2014 the new first step. Reads the optional header via `ServerHttpConnection`. If the key already names an existing owner, it republishes that owner and takes the `replay` branch (\u2192 200); otherwise it publishes the key and takes `proceed` (\u2192 normal create). A stored key whose owner no longer exists is treated as unseen, so the request proceeds normally.\n\n4. **`RecordIdempotencyKey`** \u2014 runs right after `SaveOwner` (so the generated id exists), registering the new owner under its key; a no-op when no key was supplied.\n\n**Wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`): `checkIdempotency` is the entry point with two flow outputs (`replay: respondReplay`, `proceed: validate`); `respondReplay` reuses the existing `RespondWithOwner` (200); and `recordIdempotency` was inserted between `save` and `audit`.\n\nThe repeat create short-circuits *before* the duplicate check (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`), so the second identical request returns the original owner with 200 instead of the 409 it would otherwise get \u2014 exactly what `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` asserts.",
